-- A host's thing held by a `with`, the same for every effect: an open
-- file, a socket, a process, a canvas -- what must be let go of.  Each
-- has its own `with`: `withProcess`, `Net.withConnection`, std.file's
-- `with f := File.open path do`, and so on.  In a `do` block a `with` is
-- a statement, `with withProcess c as p do`, the statements under it its
-- body.  Each one:
--
-- 1. acquires the thing;
-- 2. runs the body with it (`scoped`'s, a `With s r` in a region `s` of
--    its own);
-- 3. lets go of it when the body ends;
-- 4. comes to what the body came to.
--
-- The thing is in the custody of the runtime, as a custodian's are (the
-- `custodian` module): it lives as long as the program can reach it.
-- Letting go of it, a `with` ends it there where it held it alone; where
-- the body gave it to another -- a thread spawned in the body, given the
-- `With s r` by value -- it is ended once the last holder lets go of it,
-- the `with` not waiting.  That is `scoped`'s policy.  A thing let go of
-- by a computation of the program's, a `held`'s, has the other: it is
-- let go of as the block is left, whoever holds it still.
--
-- ```haskell
-- import co
-- import exception
-- import std.process
--
-- ran : {e : HFunctor}
--     -> {{sys : process <: e}}
--     -> {{fx : exception ProcessError <: e}}
--     -> Invocation
--     -> Co e Ran
-- ran c = do
--   with withProcess c as p do
--     wait p
-- ```
--
-- The body is polymorphic in `s`, and what it comes to is of a type bound
-- outside it, so a `With s r` cannot leave the body:
-- `withProcess c (\p -> val p)` is a type error (E0311).
--
-- There is one `With` for every effect, so a `with` of one effect may
-- hand its thing to an operation of another by value -- a
-- `<canvas>` found by browser.dom, drawn on by browser.gpu -- and neither
-- keeps it.
--
-- A `with` comes to what its body came to, and nothing else: a thing not
-- acquired is no answer of the body's, so it is thrown, over the
-- `exception` effect, the body not run -- a `with` asks for `exception
-- err` besides its own effect, and `try` can catch it.  A release that
-- fails is no answer of the body's either, nor the caller's to catch: it
-- is a failure of the runtime's, said, and the program ends, whether the
-- `with` ended the thing or its last holder did, later.
-- `held` is the `with` of a thing that is always had and let go of by
-- a computation -- a port of a standard stream, a handle on the page's
-- document -- which throws nothing; its release is kept by the
-- program's top level meanwhile, and runs however the block is left.
--
-- The thing is the host's own, a foreign value with a finalizer, so a
-- body left otherwise -- a throw heard outside it, a handler between that
-- drops the continuation -- lets go of it where the program drops its
-- last reference to it; `scoped` adds nothing for that.
--
-- `With` is exposed without its tag: no program makes one, and `scoped
-- acquire release body` and `held acquire release body` are the only ways
-- to make one in a region of its own; `beside w x` has `x` in the region
-- `w`'s thing is had in, as std.net's `accepted` has a connection in its
-- listener's.  A library wraps one around its own operations acquiring
-- and releasing the thing, as std.process does:
--
-- ```haskell
-- withProcess : {e : HFunctor} -> {a : Type} -> {{ev : process <: e}}
--             -> {{ex : exception ProcessError <: e}}
--             -> Invocation
--             -> ({s : Type} -> With s Started -> Co e a)
--             -> Co e a
-- withProcess c body = scoped (op (ev (Start (spawnOf c) (\s -> s))) val)
--                             (\s -> op (ev (Release s (\r -> r))) val) body
--
-- wait : {e : HFunctor} -> {s : Type} -> {{ev : process <: e}}
--      -> With s Started -> Co e Ran
-- wait p = op (ev (Wait (inside p) (\r -> r))) val
-- ```
--
-- - Its operations take the `With s a`, and give the thing to a provider
--   operation by `inside`, which takes it out for that operation and
--   nothing else.
-- - It may export its bare acquire and release too, as std.file does
--   (`open`, `close`; its `with` of a binding, `with f := File.open path
--   do`, is `scoped` around them, its body given the file itself): where the provider lets go of a thing once,
--   an operation on it after is the effect's error (a file's, `EBADF`),
--   never one on a thing acquired since, and its operations then take
--   the thing itself.
-- - What lives as long as the program, such as a GPU device, is held by a
--   `with` at the top of `main`.
--
-- The provider's side, how it opens and closes the thing, is its scoped
-- operations.

module resource (With, scoped, held, had, inside, beside)

import co
import custodian as Custodian
import exception

-- | A host's thing, held in the region `s` by a `with` (`scoped`,
-- | `held`).
data With (s : Type) a = MkWith a

-- | The thing a `with` holds, for a provider operation given it: a
-- | library's operation on a `With s a` takes it out here and gives it
-- | to the operation, and nothing else does.
inside : {s : Type} -> {a : Type} -> With s a -> a
inside (MkWith x) = x

-- | A thing had in the region `s` that `w`'s thing is had in: for a
-- | thing a library gets by another's -- a connection by its listener
-- | -- which lives no longer than that one's `with`, and is let go of
-- | by the library's own operation, or by the program dropping it.
beside : {s : Type} -> {a b : Type} -> With s a -> b -> With s b
beside w x = MkWith x

-- (what the body came to, once the thing is let go of; a release that
-- failed a failure of the program's, as the runtime's are)
releasedTo : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {err a : Type}
           -> {{ex : exception err <: e}}
           -> a
           -> Either err Unit
           -> Co e a
releasedTo v (Right u) = pure v
releasedTo v (Left err) = abandon

-- (the body run with the thing, then the thing released; a thing not
-- acquired, thrown)
heldBy : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {err r a : Type}
       -> {{ex : exception err <: e}}
       -> (r -> Co e (Either err Unit))
       -> ({s : Type} -> With s r -> Co e a)
       -> Either err r
       -> Co e a
heldBy release body (Left err) = throw err
heldBy release body (Right x) =
  bind
    (body (s := r) (MkWith x))
    (\v -> bind (release x) (releasedTo v))

-- | The thing `acquire` gives, held by `body` in a region of its own --
-- | it is not kept past it -- and let go of by `release` when `body`
-- | ends: what it came to.  `release` is the `with` letting go of its
-- | hold, the provider's to say: it ends the thing where the `with` held
-- | it alone, and else leaves it to the last holder, ended as that one
-- | lets go of it -- a thread the body gave it to.  A thing not acquired
-- | is thrown, the body not run; one not released, whenever it is
-- | ended, is a failure of the runtime's, as the provider says, the
-- | program ended (a `release` that comes to a `Left` abandons).  Should `body` be left otherwise -- a throw
-- | heard outside it, a handler between that drops the continuation --
-- | the thing is let go of where the program drops its last reference
-- | to it, as its provider says.
scoped : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {err r a : Type}
       -> {{ex : exception err <: e}}
       -> Co e (Either err r)
       -> (r -> Co e (Either err Unit))
       -> ({s : Type} -> With s r -> Co e a)
       -> Co e a
scoped acquire release body = bind acquire (heldBy release body)

-- (the body run with the thing, its release kept by the program's top
-- level meanwhile; then the release dropped there, and run here)
letGoAfter : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {r a : Type}
           -> {{cu : Custodian.custodian <: e}}
           -> (r -> Co e Unit)
           -> ({s : Type} -> With s r -> Co e a)
           -> r
           -> Co e a
letGoAfter release body x = do
  t <- Custodian.kept (release x)
  v <- body (s := r) (MkWith x)
  Custodian.dropped t
  release x
  pure v

-- | The thing `acquire` gives, which it always does, held by `body` in
-- | a region of its own and let go of by `release` as the block is
-- | left, whoever holds it still: what the body came to.  The release
-- | is a computation, kept meanwhile by the program's top level (the
-- | custodian's `kept`): where the block is left otherwise -- a throw
-- | heard outside it, a handler between that drops the continuation --
-- | the runtime runs it once that is known, at the next operation of
-- | the domain that kept it (C), or as the collector comes to it
-- | (JavaScript).  The `with` of a port of a standard stream, or of the
-- | page's document.
held : {e : HFunctor}
     -> {r a : Type}
     -> {{cu : Custodian.custodian <: e}}
     -> Co e r
     -> (r -> Co e Unit)
     -> ({s : Type} -> With s r -> Co e a)
     -> Co e a
held acquire release body = bind acquire (letGoAfter release body)

-- | `x`, held by `body` in a region of its own, nothing done as it
-- | ends: for a library whose thing is let go of otherwise, after the
-- | body, by an operation of its own.
had : {e : HFunctor}
    -> {r a : Type}
    -> r
    -> ({s : Type} -> With s r -> Co e a)
    -> Co e a
had x body = body (s := r) (MkWith x)
